38: /**
39:  * A <b>State Backend</b> defines how the state of a streaming application is stored locally within
40:  * the cluster. Different State Backends store their state in different fashions, and use different
41:  * data structures to hold the state of a running application.
42:  *
43:  * <p>For example, the {@link org.apache.flink.runtime.state.hashmap.HashMapStateBackend hashmap
44:  * state backend} keeps working state in the memory of the TaskManager. The backend is lightweight
45:  * and without additional dependencies.
46:  *
47:  * <p>The {@code EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend} stores working state in an embedded <a
48:  * href="http://rocksdb.org/">RocksDB</a> and is able to scale working state to many terabytes in
49:  * size, only limited by available disk space across all task managers.
50:  *
51:  * <h2>Raw Bytes Storage and Backends</h2>
52:  *
53:  * <p>The {@code StateBackend} creates services for <i>keyed state</i> and <i>operator state</i>.
54:  *
55:  * <p>The {@link CheckpointableKeyedStateBackend} and {@link OperatorStateBackend} created by this
56:  * state backend define how to hold the working state for keys and operators. They also define how
57:  * to checkpoint that state, frequently using the raw bytes storage (via the {@code
58:  * CheckpointStreamFactory}). However, it is also possible that for example a keyed state backend
59:  * simply implements the bridge to a key/value store, and that it does not need to store anything in
60:  * the raw byte storage upon a checkpoint.
61:  *
62:  * <h2>Serializability</h2>
63:  *
64:  * <p>State Backends need to be {@link java.io.Serializable serializable}, because they distributed
65:  * across parallel processes (for distributed execution) together with the streaming application
66:  * code.
67:  *
68:  * <p>Because of that, {@code StateBackend} implementations (typically subclasses of {@link
69:  * AbstractStateBackend}) are meant to be like <i>factories</i> that create the proper states stores
70:  * that provide access to the persistent storage and hold the keyed- and operator state data
71:  * structures. That way, the State Backend can be very lightweight (contain only configurations)
72:  * which makes it easier to be serializable.
73:  *
74:  * <h2>Thread Safety</h2>
75:  *
76:  * <p>State backend implementations have to be thread-safe. Multiple threads may be creating
77:  * keyed-/operator state backends concurrently.
78:  */
